---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../utils/url';
---

<Base title="About" description="Background, and how I work: applied ML grounded in physics, now owning production AI.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Background, and how I think</h1>
    </section>

    <div class="prose measure" style="margin-top: 1.5rem">
      <p>
        I started in physics, and it still shapes how I work. Physics teaches you to find the constraint that actually binds, to distrust a model
        you cannot test, and to keep a clear line between what you measured and what you assumed. That habit transfers directly to applied machine
        learning.
      </p>
      <p>
        I began as a machine learning engineer and moved into owning production AI as a technical and product lead. Today that means applied AI in
        healthcare analytics: multi-agent systems, retrieval, and the evaluation work that tells you whether any of it is getting better. The
        <a href={url('/work/enterprise-ai-analytics-platform')}>platform case study</a> is the clearest picture of that work.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Foundations before tickets.</strong> I write down the architecture, the data model and the failure modes first. Tickets are cheap once the shape is agreed, and expensive when it is not.</li>
        <li><strong>Questions to find constraints.</strong> Most of the useful design information is what cannot change: a network boundary, a data policy, an approval process, a user’s habit. I ask until I know where those are.</li>
        <li><strong>Abstractions grounded in practical limits.</strong> A clean design that ignores latency, cost, permissions or how people actually work is a draft. I test every abstraction against those limits before I commit to it.</li>
        <li><strong>Say what stage it is.</strong> Shipped, partly built and designed are different things, and I label them that way.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Outside work</h2>
